Description: Midwire system configuration and updater
 The parts of the Midwire USB proxy appliance that belong to the system
 rather than to one program: the apt source and signing key for Midwire's
 own packages, the updater that the web UI's Updates page drives, and the
 rule that keeps NetworkManager off network interfaces on USB devices.
 .
 It also holds the helper behind the web UI's SSH settings: turning SSH on
 or off, setting a login user's password and managing that user's SSH keys.
 Only the web UI's service user can reach it, over a local socket.
 .
 While the proxy is idle it turns the USB-C port into a network adapter, so
 the computer on the other end of the cable can open the web UI. The port
 goes back to the proxy when the proxy starts.
 .
 It decides what the Pi's Wi-Fi does: join a saved network when one is in
 range, host the Midwire hotspot when none is, and go back to the saved
 network when it returns.
 .
 The updater upgrades only Midwire's packages (this one, midwire-web-ui,
 usb-proxy and raw-gadget-dkms). The operating system is left alone. It
 checks once a day and installs only when asked.
 .
 Installing this package pulls in the rest of Midwire.
